<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Archaeological Survey of India</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as ASI</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Archaeological Survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> is the Indian government agency responsible for archaeological research and for conserving the country's historic monuments. It grew out of earlier study by the Asiatic Society, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1784, and James Prinsep's decipherment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> in 1837. Alexander Cunningham, who had excavated stupas across India, lobbied for a permanent body, and the survey was formed in 1861 by a statute under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-canning-1st-earl-canning">Lord Canning</a> with Cunningham as its first Archaeological Surveyor. Suspended for lack of funds from 1865, it was revived in 1871 as a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> with Cunningham as Director-General.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…described the mounds of Harappa in a narrative of his travels published in 1842; Alexander Cunningham of the <b>Archaeological Survey of India</b> published the first Indus seal in 1875 without recognising its age; and in 1857 builders of the Lahore–Multan railway carried off bricks from Harappa as ballast.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Adichanallur first drew attention in 1876, and Alexander Rea of the <b>Archaeological Survey of India</b> investigated it between 1899 and 1905.</blockquote><a href="/culture/south-indian-megalithic-culture">South Indian megalithic culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Archaeological Survey of India</b> worked on the temple from 1986 to 1992, and both its work and the early French restorations have been criticised for the damage done by chemicals and cement.</blockquote><a href="/work/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Since independence in 1947 the <b>Archaeological Survey of India</b> has maintained it, and in 1983 it became a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/work/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
